Here's some JavaScript that you can use to record the webpage URL and timestamp in local storage during a website visit:
```
// Get the current webpage URL
var url = window.location.href;

// Get the current timestamp
var timestamp = new Date().getTime();

// Store the URL and timestamp in local storage
localStorage.setItem('url', url);
localStorage.setItem('timestamp', timestamp);
```
You can then retrieve the stored URL and timestamp from local storage like this:
```
// Get the stored URL and timestamp from local storage
var storedUrl = localStorage.getItem('url');
var storedTimestamp = localStorage.getItem('timestamp');

console.log(storedUrl);
console.log(storedTimestamp);
```
Keep in mind that local storage is accessible only to the domain that stored the data, and that the data is persisted even after the user closes the browser.

Here's some modified JavaScript code that will store the webpage URL and timestamp in a single JSON object in local storage, with the key "us\_main":
```
// Create an object to store the URL and timestamp
var data = {
  url: window.location.href,
  timestamp: new Date().getTime()
};

// Convert the object to a JSON string
var jsonData = JSON.stringify(data);

// Store the JSON string in local storage
localStorage.setItem('us_main', jsonData);
```
You can then retrieve the stored data from local storage like this:
```
// Get the stored JSON string from local storage
var storedJsonData = localStorage.getItem('us_main');

// Convert the JSON string back into an object
var storedData = JSON.parse(storedJsonData);

console.log(storedData.url);
console.log(storedData.timestamp);
```
Keep in mind that local storage is accessible only to the domain that stored the data, and that the data is persisted even after the user closes the browser.

React components are JavaScript functions or classes that return markup (HTML, XML, etc.) describing what should be rendered on the screen.

In a functional component, the component is defined as a JavaScript function that takes in some properties (props) as input, and returns some markup as output. For example:
```javascript
function MyComponent(props) {
  return <div>Hello {props.name}!</div>;
}
```
In a class component, the component is defined as a JavaScript class that extends the `React.Component` base class, and has a `render()` method that returns some markup. For example:
```javascript
class MyComponent extends React.Component {
  render() {
    return <div>Hello {this.props.name}!</div>;
  }
}
```
Both of these components can be used in a React application by rendering them to the DOM using the `ReactDOM.render()` function. For example:
```javascript
ReactDOM.render(<MyComponent name="John" />, document.getElementById('root'));
```
This will render the `MyComponent` component with a `name` prop of "John" to the element with an `id` of "root" in the HTML document.

