An Apex trigger is a piece of Apex code that runs automatically in response to a specific event, such as the creation or update of a record. Triggers allow you to automate custom business logic in Salesforce.

Here's an example of an Apex trigger that updates a custom field on the `Account` object whenever a new account is created:
```csharp
trigger UpdateAccountField on Account (before insert) {
    for (Account acc : Trigger.new) {
        acc.Custom_Field__c = 'Value for new accounts';
    }
}
```
In this example, the trigger is defined using the `trigger` keyword, followed by the name of the trigger (`UpdateAccountField`) and the object it's associated with (`Account`). The trigger is set to run `before insert`, which means it will run before a new `Account` record is inserted into the database.

The trigger consists of a `for` loop that iterates over the `Trigger.new` collection, which contains the new `Account` records being inserted. For each `Account` record in the collection, the trigger updates the value of the `Custom_Field__c` field to `'Value for new accounts'`.

When a new `Account` record is inserted, this trigger will run automatically and update the value of the `Custom_Field__c` field as specified. This is just one simple example of what can be done with Apex triggers. They can also be used for more complex business logic, such as sending notifications, updating related records, or calling external web services.

Governor limits are a set of limits in Apex that control the amount of system resources that can be consumed by a single transaction. These limits help ensure that the performance of the overall Salesforce platform is not impacted by a single resource-intensive transaction.

Here's an example of how governor limits can affect the behavior of an Apex class:
```csharp
public class MyClass {
    public static void doSomething() {
        List<Account> accounts = [SELECT Name FROM Account LIMIT 50000];
        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            acc.Name = acc.Name + ' Updated';
            update acc;
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, the `doSomething` method retrieves a list of up to 50,000 `Account` records and updates the `Name` field of each record by appending the text `' Updated'`. However, this code will exceed the governor limit for the number of records that can be processed in a single transaction, which is typically around 10,000 records.

As a result, when this code is executed, a `System.LimitException` will be thrown and the transaction will be terminated. To avoid this issue, the code could be modified to process the records in smaller batches, or to use an asynchronous process (such as a batch class) to update the records in a separate transaction.

By understanding and working within the governor limits, you can ensure that your Apex code runs efficiently and reliably, and that it does not impact the performance of the overall Salesforce platform.

